DwarfSpec TestBed Implementation
================================

Purpose:
    ☐ Implement the loader-centered TestBed defined by docs/testbed-proposal.md.
    ☐ Deliver a framework-neutral private Lua module and script graph for standalone tests.
    ☐ Integrate fresh, mount-owned TestBeds only for tagged ds.mount component-source descriptors.
    ☐ Preserve ordinary class mounting, DwarfSpec run-level path handling, and existing resource cleanup behavior.

Completion contract:
    ☐ Treat this checklist and docs/testbed-proposal.md as the implementation contract; if they disagree, stop and reconcile the documents before coding the disputed behavior.
    ☐ Complete implementation, focused tests, full tests, declarations, packaging, downstream installed-rock proof, live DFHack proof, documentation, and cleanup.
    ☐ Do not mark a phase complete merely because its production code exists; satisfy its stated exit gate and record the evidence.
    ☐ Keep offline unit evidence, declaration evidence, package evidence, and live installed-runtime evidence distinct.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash, commit, reset, or overwrite them.
    ☐ Add language-standard documentation comments to every new or changed class, public method, internal method, and callable module API.
    ☐ Keep the framework-neutral entry point free of DFHack, DwarfSpec driver, scheduler, ds, and Busted dependencies.

Scope boundaries:
    ☐ Implement TestBed as an opt-in per-example composition and loader boundary, not as a complete Lua VM or security sandbox.
    ☐ Do not promise rollback of borrowed-table writes, native DF state, host caches, event registries, timers, files, screens, or plugin state.
    ☐ Do not add diagnostic graph inspection, reload APIs, provider factories, class providers, dependency arrays, scopes, multi-providers, automatic mocks, or Busted convenience adapters.
    ☐ Do not support legacy reqscript moduleMode behavior.
    ☐ Do not emulate live script-path integration, mod activation, save-specific paths, or DFHack file-change hot reload inside TestBed.
    ☐ Do not impose physical path-containment or symlink-containment rules beyond normal Lua loading semantics.
    ☐ Do not accept an already-created component instance, an instantiated TestBed in ds.mount, or TestBed configuration on an ordinary class mount.
    ☐ Do not replace DwarfSpec's existing run-level consumer path and module-eviction behavior.

Phase 0: Freeze the implementation map and baseline
    ☐ Exit with the approved proposal mapped to exact production, test, declaration, packaging, and live-validation seams, and with the pre-change baseline recorded.

0.1 Contract inventory:
    ☐ Convert every item under "Recommended delivery" and "Prototype acceptance criteria" in docs/testbed-proposal.md into a traceable checklist row or explicit out-of-scope row here.
    ☐ Record the required public symbols: dwarfspec.testbed, TestBed.new, TestBed:require, TestBed:reqscript, TestBed:close, and the tagged ds.mount overload.
    ☐ Record all frozen semantic exceptions to native Lua: reserved dfhack resolution, bounded unpublished require cycles, unavailable native cpath/loadlib, protected loader-owned facade fields, and shared close-state checks.
    ☐ Record the intentional Lua-version boundary: dedicated standalone conformance on Lua 5.4; Lua 5.3 supported but untested by a dedicated suite.
    ☐ Record diagnostic graph inspection as deferred so no public or provisional inspection API enters the implementation accidentally.

0.2 Repository seam audit:
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed.lua as the framework-neutral public entry point.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/config.lua for validation, normalization, copied containers, and the frozen provider registry.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/paths.lua for module path construction, searchpath compatibility, and script/source resolution.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/providers.lua for exact-token strategy and alias resolution.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/base_environment.lua for standard bindings, global facades, protected operations, and bound dynamic loaders.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/package_state.lua for private package state, require, caches, searchers, loader data, cycles, and close checks.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/testbed/script_loader.lua for modern reqscript annotation, environments, caches, providers, cycles, and retry behavior.
    ☐ Refine these internal seams before implementation if repository conventions or cyclic dependencies require a smaller split; record the replacement mapping in this checklist.
    ☐ Confirm src/dwarfspec/driver/mount/testbed_adapter.lua as the only live adapter from the framework-neutral core to the active DwarfSpec project and host loaders.
    ☐ Map descriptor dispatch and lifetime ownership through driver/commands/mount.lua, driver/mount/mount_context.lua, driver/mount/mount_resource_ownership.lua, driver/mount/component.lua, and driver/mount/mount_cleanup_verification.lua.
    ☐ Map the canonical declaration surface through production annotations and src/ds.d.lua without duplicating TestBed types.
    ☐ Select exact unit, declaration, integration, downstream-consumer, automation, and package-audit fixture locations before adding files.

0.3 Baseline evidence:
    ☐ Record git status and distinguish pre-existing changes from work performed by this checklist.
    ☐ Run the focused existing mount command, mount context, component, ownership, and cleanup-verification unit specs.
    ☐ Run tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1 and record the baseline result.
    ☐ Run tools/Check-Lua.ps1 and record the baseline result.
    ☐ Record any pre-existing failure without weakening later exit gates.

Phase 1: Establish canonical authoring types and the framework-neutral module boundary
    ☐ Exit with the public type vocabulary and load-safe module skeleton present, documented, and testable without DFHack.

1.1 Canonical runtime annotations:
    ☐ Add dwarfspec.TestBedImportKind with only "module" and "script".
    ☐ Add dwarfspec.TestBedImportToken with kind and logical name.
    ☐ Add the non-nil value alias required to type use_value=false correctly.
    ☐ Add value, source, host, and existing provider variants and the dwarfspec.TestBedImport union.
    ☐ Add dwarfspec.TestBedGlobals, dwarfspec.TestBedConfig, and dwarfspec.TestBed.
    ☐ Annotate TestBed.new(config?), TestBed:require(name), TestBed:reqscript(name), and TestBed:close().
    ☐ Annotate TestBed:require with a second optional loader-data return instead of pretending every supported Lua version returns it.
    ☐ Add documentation comments describing ownership, borrowing, mutability, and failure behavior for every public API.

1.2 Entry-point dependency boundary:
    ☐ Add src/dwarfspec/testbed.lua without importing df, dfhack, gui, DwarfSpec host, scheduler, mount, ds, or Busted modules.
    ☐ Keep live capabilities injectable through a private adapter contract rather than discovered from process globals by the standalone entry point.
    ☐ Ensure requiring dwarfspec.testbed does not modify process package.path, package.preload, or unrelated package.loaded entries.
    ☐ Add a focused module-load test that removes DFHack globals and asserts that require('dwarfspec.testbed') succeeds.
    ☐ Add a dependency-spy test proving the entry point does not load live-only or test-framework modules.

1.3 Declaration ownership:
    ☐ Make production TestBed annotations authoritative.
    ☐ Define dwarfspec.ComponentClass, dwarfspec.ModuleComponentSource, dwarfspec.ScriptComponentSource, and dwarfspec.TestBedComponentSource at the appropriate public declaration surface.
    ☐ Update src/ds.d.lua to reference canonical TestBed types instead of declaring a second provider/config schema.
    ☐ Express the two-argument ordinary class overload separately from the optional-third-argument descriptor overload.

Phase 2: Validate and freeze TestBed configuration
    ☐ Exit with a configuration normalizer that accepts the canonical schema, rejects ambiguous input, and produces an immutable normalized construction snapshot ready for TestBed.new in Phase 9.

2.1 Top-level validation and defaults:
    ☐ Accept nil as an empty configuration.
    ☐ Reject non-table configuration values and unknown top-level fields.
    ☐ Validate module_roots and script_roots as dense arrays of strings.
    ☐ Validate globals as a table and component_imports as a boolean when present.
    ☐ Validate globals.dfhack as a table when present.
    ☐ Reject configured replacements for _G, require, reqscript, mkmodule, package, load, loadfile, dofile, reload, script_environment, and dfhack_flags.
    ☐ Apply offline defaults: module_roots ordered as src/scripts_modinstalled, src, and .; script_roots as src/scripts_modinstalled; component_imports=false; imports empty.
    ☐ Treat explicit root arrays, including empty arrays, as replacements rather than additions to defaults.
    ☐ Establish one effective consumer root at construction; framework-neutral TestBed.new uses the process current directory and the live adapter supplies the active consumer project root.
    ☐ Skip missing default directories while retaining their logical order in diagnostics where useful.

2.2 Provider validation:
    ☐ Validate imports as a dense ordered array of provider tables.
    ☐ Reject unknown provider fields and require every provide token kind/name to use the canonical kind and a non-empty string name.
    ☐ Require exactly one raw-present strategy key among use_value, use_source, use_host, and use_existing.
    ☐ Recognize use_value=false and reject use_value=nil as an absent/invalid strategy.
    ☐ Require script use_value payloads to be tables while allowing any non-nil Lua module value.
    ☐ Require use_host to be exactly true.
    ☐ Validate use_source as a non-empty source path string.
    ☐ Validate use_existing as a canonical non-empty token in the same namespace as provide.
    ☐ Reject duplicate user-provided tokens.
    ☐ Reject every provider whose provide token is {kind='module', name='dfhack'}.
    ☐ Reject component_imports=true and all use_host providers when no live importer is supplied.

2.3 Frozen construction state:
    ☐ Copy the configuration table, provider array, provider tables, tokens, roots, and globals container at construction.
    ☐ Deliberately borrow use_value payloads and nested values supplied inside globals; document that their later mutation is observable.
    ☐ Build an immutable normalized provider registry keyed by exact (kind, name) identity.
    ☐ Prevent later mutation of caller-owned configuration containers from changing the bed.
    ☐ Keep private runtime package state mutable and separate from the frozen normalized configuration.

2.4 Focused verification:
    ☐ Add table-driven tests for every valid omission and invalid field/type combination.
    ☐ Test explicit empty roots, root replacement, provider order, duplicate keys, same text in different namespaces, false values, nil values, and script table constraints.
    ☐ Test copied containers and borrowed payload identities independently.
    ☐ Test clear actionable errors that identify the failing configuration path.

Phase 3: Build deterministic path and source resolution
    ☐ Exit with isolated module/script search rules that match the proposal without mutating host paths or inventing containment guarantees.

3.1 Module roots and private path:
    ☐ Resolve every relative default or explicitly configured module root against the effective consumer root, never the DwarfSpec checkout.
    ☐ Normalize absolute and resolved module roots without imposing physical containment.
    ☐ Expand every effective module root into ordinary ?.lua and ?/init.lua templates in order.
    ☐ Construct the initial private package.path without changing process package.path.
    ☐ Provide a compatible private package.searchpath implementation over an explicitly supplied or private path.
    ☐ Preserve normal Lua path-template behavior, including explicit paths, parent traversal, and symlink traversal.

3.2 Script roots:
    ☐ Resolve every relative default or explicitly configured script root against the effective consumer root.
    ☐ Resolve logical script names deterministically across script_roots using the documented DFHack-style filename mapping.
    ☐ Keep the script search namespace separate from ordinary modules even when names are textually equal.
    ☐ Resolve relative use_source paths against the effective consumer root and retain absolute use_source paths unchanged.
    ☐ Ensure use_source paths load exactly that resolved file instead of being remapped through roots.
    ☐ Report missing scripts with the logical name, effective roots, and attempted candidates.

3.3 Focused verification:
    ☐ Test default-root order, missing default directories, explicit replacement roots, empty roots, ?.lua, and ?/init.lua.
    ☐ Test private package.path replacement and mutation affecting only later loads in that bed.
    ☐ Test standalone-current-directory and live-supplied roots for relative module_roots, script_roots, and use_source paths.
    ☐ Test explicit absolute source paths and symlink-capable behavior without a containment rejection.
    ☐ Test missing-module errors that report the effective project root/current directory, effective roots, attempted resolution, and bounded dependency chain.
    ☐ Assert process package.path remains byte-for-byte unchanged.

Phase 4: Construct the TestBed base environment and protected facades
    ☐ Exit with stable bed-owned base and dfhack facade factories that accept later package/loader closures, expose only documented base bindings, and never fall through dynamically to host state.

4.1 Standard-Lua baseline:
    ☐ Copy only _VERSION, assert, collectgarbage, error, getmetatable, ipairs, next, pairs, pcall, print, rawequal, rawget, rawlen, rawset, select, setmetatable, tonumber, tostring, type, xpcall, coroutine, debug, io, math, os, string, table, and utf8 from the running interpreter.
    ☐ Include warn only when supplied by Lua 5.4.
    ☐ Populate a bed-owned base facade rather than copying process _G wholesale.
    ☐ Borrow documented standard library references while keeping bed-owned global assignments off process _G.
    ☐ Reserve package, require, reqscript, mkmodule, load, loadfile, and dofile bindings for explicit bed-owned closures supplied during later graph assembly.
    ☐ Install bed-owned rawget, rawset, next, and pairs wrapper behavior at the base-facade layer.
    ☐ Retain the running interpreter's ordinary getmetatable and setmetatable functions; protect the base facade with native __metatable behavior instead of custom wrappers.
    ☐ Protect reserved loader-owned names from replacement through ordinary assignment and bed-local rawset.

4.2 Offline and live base construction:
    ☐ Use an empty ordinary-API dfhack backing when offline and globals.dfhack is omitted.
    ☐ In live construction, shallow-snapshot every non-reserved raw binding from the active dfhack.BASE_G at bed creation.
    ☐ Borrow values from that snapshot without dynamically reading host additions or replacements later.
    ☐ Apply configured globals after the baseline so they replace ordinary non-reserved bindings.
    ☐ Treat globals.dfhack as the complete ordinary-API backing; omitted members must not fall through to the live host dfhack object.

4.3 dfhack and BASE_G identity:
    ☐ Create one stable TestBed-owned dfhack facade for the initial global binding and expose that exact identity to the reserved require resolver added in Phase 6.
    ☐ Create one stable mutable bed-owned dfhack.BASE_G facade/backing shared throughout the bed.
    ☐ Make ordinary writes to non-reserved dfhack.BASE_G fields visible bed-wide without modifying real host base state.
    ☐ Protect the base facade metatable from ordinary replacement.
    ☐ Protect reserved dfhack and BASE_G loader fields from ordinary assignment and bed-local rawset.
    ☐ Make bed-local rawget, rawset, next, and pairs recognize the base facade and operate on its backing for ordinary fields.
    ☐ Make writes to ordinary dfhack facade fields affect the selected backing table while retaining the reserved-field protections.
    ☐ Mask host reload, dfhack.reload, script_environment, dfhack.script_environment, and every other versioned reserved loader entry point with documented unsupported-operation errors.
    ☐ Centralize and publish the supported-DFHack-version reserved-loader policy so live snapshot filtering, base bindings, facade delegation, validation, documentation, and tests cannot drift.
    ☐ Make unbound dfhack.BASE_G.load, loadfile, and dofile fail clearly instead of using an ambiguous environment.

4.4 Focused verification:
    ☐ Test offline and live snapshots separately.
    ☐ Test facade stability, backing completeness, no host fallthrough, bed-wide mutable ordinary fields, and unchanged host tables with controlled loader-closure inputs.
    ☐ Test normal writes, rawget/rawset, next/pairs enumeration, and metatable replacement attempts for every protected category.
    ☐ Test that later process-global and host-base additions are invisible.
    ☐ Install host sentinels for reload, dfhack.reload, script_environment, dfhack.script_environment, and every supported-version reserved loader field; prove TestBed returns its documented implementation or rejection without invoking a sentinel.
    ☐ Fail the reserved-policy test if any supported-version entry lacks base-facade, dfhack-facade, validation, and live-snapshot behavior.

Phase 5: Create module-environment and dynamic-loader primitives
    ☐ Exit with an environment factory that can receive bed-owned loader closures and execute source without touching process globals; do not claim complete require behavior in this phase.

5.1 Module environments:
    ☐ Create a fresh environment for each source-loaded module.
    ☐ Bind environment _G to the module environment itself.
    ☐ Fall back from the module environment to the bed-owned base facade.
    ☐ Ensure direct global writes and _G-qualified writes target the same module environment and never process _G.
    ☐ Accept the owning private package, require, reqscript, mkmodule, load, loadfile, and dofile closures as explicit environment-factory inputs.
    ☐ Do not publish an ordinary source-module environment into package.loaded merely because the environment was allocated.
    ☐ Do not inject dfhack_flags into ordinary module environments.

5.2 Bound dynamic loaders:
    ☐ Match native load and loadfile signatures and return conventions for the running Lua version.
    ☐ Default load and loadfile to the environment that owns the closure.
    ☐ Honor an explicit environment argument exactly.
    ☐ Make dofile execute in the owning environment and preserve all chunk return values.
    ☐ Ensure dynamically loaded code receives the same bed-local dependency closures.
    ☐ Verify nested load, loadfile, and dofile operations do not modify host globals or paths.

5.3 Environment-factory verification:
    ☐ Test direct and _G-qualified writes, base fallback, injected loader identity, absence of dfhack_flags, and process-global isolation with controlled loader doubles.
    ☐ Test load, loadfile, and dofile default environments, explicit environments, syntax errors, file errors, and multiple return values.
    ☐ Defer transitive source-module, cache, provider, and circular-import claims to Phases 6 and 7.

Phase 6: Implement the private package, bed-local require, and mkmodule
    ☐ Exit with Lua-compatible non-reserved source-module loading through the Phase 5 environment factory, deterministic loader data, private mutable package state, mkmodule, and bounded dependency failures.

6.1 Private package state:
    ☐ Create bed-owned package.loaded, package.preload, package.searchers, package.path, package.config, and package.searchpath fields.
    ☐ Match package.config and package.searchpath behavior to the running Lua version.
    ☐ Retain bounded internal source-selection and result records needed for deterministic errors without exposing a public graph-inspection API.
    ☐ Initialize searchers in the documented order: private preload, exact-provider integration point, then Lua source.
    ☐ Make entry mutations in loaded and preload authoritative for later non-reserved requires.
    ☐ Make replacement or mutation of searchers and path authoritative for later non-reserved requires.
    ☐ Match native Lua behavior when code assigns a different table to package.loaded or package.preload: retain the internal cache/preload tables used by require.
    ☐ Provide no working package.cpath or package.loadlib route to host native loading.

6.2 Require algorithm:
    ☐ Validate every TestBed:require name as a non-empty string before cache or searcher access.
    ☐ Resolve module name dfhack to the exact facade installed as the initial global before consulting cache, preload, searchers, providers, or paths.
    ☐ Apply running-Lua cache semantics for all non-reserved names, including false values and loader-written package.loaded values.
    ☐ Invoke mutable searchers in order and aggregate their errors in native-compatible form with the TestBed dependency chain.
    ☐ Pass each searcher's loader data to its loader as the second argument.
    ☐ Compile every root-loaded Lua source file through the Phase 5 owning-environment factory.
    ☐ Preserve loader return values and package.loaded decisions according to native require behavior.
    ☐ On Lua 5.4, return deterministic loader data only when a searcher actually loads the module; return no second result on a cache hit.
    ☐ On Lua 5.3, preserve its native return shape while retaining source compatibility.
    ☐ Use exactly ":preload:" for private preload loader data.
    ☐ Use the normalized resolved filename for root-loaded Lua source and use_source loader data.
    ☐ Use ":testbed:use_value:<kind>:<name>", ":testbed:use_host:<kind>:<name>", and ":testbed:use_existing:<kind>:<name>" for provider loader data; the alias reports its own token rather than its target's loader data.

6.3 mkmodule:
    ☐ Validate every mkmodule name as a non-empty string.
    ☐ Return a stable module environment for repeated mkmodule(name) calls within one bed.
    ☐ Immediately publish the environment into private package.loaded.
    ☐ Allow ordinary circular imports to observe that published environment.
    ☐ Return a fresh environment for the same module name in a different bed.
    ☐ Do not invent cache-conflict behavior not defined by the proposal; if implementation reveals an unresolved host-compatibility case, update the proposal before adding a rule.

6.4 Cycle, failure, and isolation verification:
    ☐ Track active non-reserved module loads by logical name and dependency chain only after ordinary cache lookup.
    ☐ Allow cycles that observe a non-nil, non-false value already published in private package.loaded.
    ☐ Fail an unpublished cycle with a deterministic bounded circular-require chain instead of waiting for a recursion-limit error.
    ☐ Clear active-loading markers on success and every error path.
    ☐ Preserve loader-written cache state on failure exactly as native Lua would; do not add an automatic rollback policy.
    ☐ Permit retry after failure whenever the ordinary cache state does not already provide a result.
    ☐ Prove two beds load the same stateful source into distinct graph state.
    ☐ Prove nested dependencies use the same owning environment and bed.
    ☐ Prove package mutations in one bed do not affect another bed or the process package tables.
    ☐ Test missing modules, malformed searchers, loader errors, false cache values, custom preload entries, custom searchers, and source shadowing.
    ☐ Prove package.loaded.dfhack mutation, package.preload.dfhack installation, replacement searchers, and matching source paths cannot redirect require('dfhack') or invoke host require.
    ☐ Document and test that externally supplied preload functions and replacement searchers retain whatever globals and dependencies their closures already captured.
    ☐ Bound dependency chains and error formatting so diagnostics never dump unbounded tables or source text.
    ☐ Add a Lua 5.4 require-conformance matrix covering cache, return, exact loader-data, and package.loaded edge cases.

Phase 7: Implement ordinary-module TestBedImport provider resolution
    ☐ Exit with all four provider strategies resolved for the module namespace through one exact-token registry, source providers using the Phase 5 environment factory, host borrowing explicit, and namespace-neutral hooks ready for Phase 8 script resolution.

7.1 Provider searcher:
    ☐ Install the ordinary-module provider searcher into the exact-provider slot established in Phase 6.
    ☐ Resolve module providers only for the exact module namespace and logical name requested.
    ☐ Keep module providers ahead of source search so an explicit provider reserves its exact name.
    ☐ Return use_value payloads by exact identity and retain native false-module reload behavior.
    ☐ Load module use_source files through the Phase 5 owning-environment factory.
    ☐ Route module use_host only through a live require importer injected at construction.
    ☐ Mark/document use_host results and applicable loader errors as borrowed host values whose host caches and side effects are outside TestBed ownership.
    ☐ Resolve module use_existing recursively in the module namespace and cache the exact target identity under the alias.

7.2 Provider cycles and failures:
    ☐ Detect module alias-only cycles with a bounded chain.
    ☐ Include the provider token and strategy in actionable resolution errors.
    ☐ Preserve ordinary module cache rules when a provider target fails.
    ☐ Prove the provider registry retains distinct module and script tokens with the same name without prematurely resolving the script token.

7.3 Native plugin behavior:
    ☐ Permit genuine plugins.* modules through explicit use_host.
    ☐ Permit plugins.* fakes through use_value.
    ☐ Permit pure-Lua plugins.* shims through use_source without invoking native loading.
    ☐ Add host-spy tests proving only explicit module use_host routes invoke host require.

Phase 8: Implement modern bed-local reqscript
    ☐ Exit with a separate script graph that supports annotated module scripts, exact providers, aliases, circular imports, and retryable failures without host cache leakage.

8.1 Script-source contract:
    ☐ Validate every TestBed:reqscript name as a non-empty string before cache, provider, or root access.
    ☐ Recognize only the modern --@ module=true annotation accepted by current DFHack plugins.
    ☐ Reject a missing annotation before executing the script.
    ☐ Reject legacy moduleMode usage with an error naming the supported annotation contract.
    ☐ Allocate the script environment immediately before execution.
    ☐ Install a raw, script-owned dfhack_flags table with module=true before execution.
    ☐ Ensure command-only code guarded by the module flag does not run.
    ☐ Return the preallocated source-script environment regardless of ordinary chunk return values.
    ☐ Apply the same annotation and environment rules to root-resolved and use_source scripts.

8.2 Script cache and namespace:
    ☐ Maintain a TestBed-owned script cache distinct from private package.loaded and process-global DFHack script caches.
    ☐ Resolve scripts in the exact order: bed cache, exact script provider, declared script_roots, then a bounded dependency-chain failure.
    ☐ Complete use_value, use_source, use_host, and use_existing provider resolution for the script namespace through the Phase 7 registry hooks.
    ☐ Route script use_host only through the injected live reqscript importer.
    ☐ Make bed:reqscript(name) and TestBed-local dfhack.reqscript(name) return the same environment identity.
    ☐ Prepublish a source script environment so supported circular script imports share it.
    ☐ Do not inject dfhack_flags into use_value, use_host, or use_existing results.
    ☐ Resolve script use_existing targets before allocating an alias environment and cache the target's exact identity.
    ☐ Detect alias-only script cycles with a bounded chain.
    ☐ Remove TestBed-preallocated script cache entries and active markers after annotation or execution failure so later calls can retry.
    ☐ Do not fall back to host reqscript unless an exact use_host provider requests it.

8.3 Focused verification:
    ☐ Prove two beds load the same source script without sharing script globals.
    ☐ Test module guards, raw flags, missing annotation, legacy moduleMode, execution failure, retry, circular imports, aliases, and provider results.
    ☐ Assert host script_environment, host reqscript, and process script caches remain untouched except for explicit use_host.
    ☐ Add a host-spy test proving only an exact script use_host provider invokes host reqscript.
    ☐ Test bounded missing-script and dependency-chain diagnostics that never dump unbounded tables or source text.

Phase 9: Complete lifecycle semantics and the public TestBed facade
    ☐ Exit with a cohesive TestBed.new/require/reqscript/close API whose ownership and closed-state behavior is uniform across every retained closure.

9.1 Public orchestration:
    ☐ Assemble normalized configuration, path state, provider registry, base facade, dfhack facade, private package, module cache, and script cache into one bed.
    ☐ Ensure no user module or script is loaded during TestBed.new.
    ☐ Keep construction atomic; discard partially created internal state if validation or setup fails.
    ☐ Keep implementation internals private and return only the documented TestBed surface.

9.2 Shared closed-state sentinel:
    ☐ Make close idempotent.
    ☐ Mark the graph closed and clear TestBed-owned cache, environment, provider, configuration, source-record, and result-record references.
    ☐ Retain only the shared closed-state sentinel and static closed-operation error metadata needed by retained loader closures; retain no inspectable graph state.
    ☐ Check the same sentinel in TestBed:require, TestBed:reqscript, and every bed-owned require, reqscript, mkmodule, load, loadfile, and dofile closure retained by loaded code.
    ☐ Return a consistent actionable closed-TestBed error from all guarded entry points.
    ☐ Leave already-created consumer values callable as ordinary caller-owned references after close.
    ☐ Do not introduce a process-global registry of active or closed TestBeds.
    ☐ Prove an unclosed standalone bed installs no process-global hooks or loader mutations, without representing that as cleanup of consumer side effects.

9.3 Core acceptance tests:
    ☐ Add a zero-configuration standalone test loading a conventional module and annotated script from a consumer project root.
    ☐ Add a configured standalone test with module and script dependencies using all non-host strategies.
    ☐ Test fresh state, nested replacement visibility, close idempotence, retained closure failures, and retained consumer values.
    ☐ Test that command-execution functions supplied through a live backing are treated as host effects rather than graph members.

Phase 10: Add the live adapter and synthesized component imports
    ☐ Exit with live construction parameters supplied explicitly by DwarfSpec and with the foundational host-module set deterministic and overridable.

10.1 Adapter inputs:
    ☐ Derive the effective consumer project root from the active run context.
    ☐ Supply the live dfhack backing and a construction-time raw dfhack.BASE_G snapshot source.
    ☐ Supply exact host require and reqscript functions without exposing them to the offline core as defaults.
    ☐ Keep all host, scheduler, run-context, and resource-registration dependencies in the driver-side adapter.

10.2 Synthesized providers:
    ☐ Define the supported DFHack version for the synthesized component-import compatibility set and keep its version mapping beside the provider list.
    ☐ When descriptor mounting defaults component_imports to true, synthesize use_host module providers for class, utils, gui, gui.widgets, and gui.dwarfmode only.
    ☐ Exclude plugins.overlay, plugins.eventful, repeat-util, script-manager, and every undocumented host module.
    ☐ Let component_imports=false remove the entire synthesized set.
    ☐ Let an explicit user provider replace a synthesized provider with the same token.
    ☐ Continue to reject duplicates among user-provided tokens.
    ☐ Preserve exact host class identity for DFHack defclass and widget construction.

10.3 Adapter verification:
    ☐ Test project-root forwarding, default enablement for mounts, explicit disablement, user override precedence, and excluded host-module failures.
    ☐ Test borrowed host identities and disclose that invoking host loaders may populate or reuse process caches.
    ☐ Test that no adapter dependency is loaded by standalone require('dwarfspec.testbed').
    ☐ Test that the declared component-import compatibility version selects exactly the documented provider set.

Phase 11: Revise ds.mount around tagged descriptors and TestBed ownership
    ☐ Exit with descriptor mounts creating exactly one fresh bed while ordinary class mounts retain their established TestBed-free behavior.

11.1 Public argument discrimination:
    ☐ Accept an ordinary callable ComponentClass only through ds.mount(component_class, options?).
    ☐ Reject already-created component instances before construction or resource registration.
    ☐ Reject a third argument on an ordinary class mount.
    ☐ Accept descriptors shaped only as {kind='module'|'script', name=string, export?=string}.
    ☐ Reject unknown descriptor fields, malformed tags, empty names, and invalid export values.
    ☐ Accept TestBed configuration only as the optional final argument of a tagged descriptor mount.
    ☐ Reject an instantiated TestBed wherever a configuration table is expected.
    ☐ Keep component constructor options and TestBed configuration as separate arguments even when their field names overlap.

11.2 Descriptor resolution:
    ☐ Create one fresh mount-owned TestBed before resolving the descriptor.
    ☐ Resolve module descriptors with bed:require(name) and script descriptors with bed:reqscript(name).
    ☐ Resolve an omitted export according to the documented direct-export rule.
    ☐ Resolve a named export from the returned module/script table and report a missing or non-component export clearly.
    ☐ Validate the resolved value as a component class before invoking its constructor.
    ☐ Ensure provider configuration is active before the component-defining module or script is loaded.
    ☐ Never require the caller to provide a physical component source path.
    ☐ Pass component constructor options to the existing component-mount boundary unchanged and return the ordinary mounted root Subject.

11.3 Ownership and unwind ordering:
    ☐ Register the bed as mount-owned immediately after successful construction of the TestBed.
    ☐ Register later component cleanup after bed cleanup so the existing LIFO drain executes component teardown first.
    ☐ On resolution or component-construction failure, close the bed exactly once.
    ☐ On mount failure after component construction, run applicable component cleanup before closing the bed.
    ☐ On normal unmount, unmount and destroy the component before closing the bed.
    ☐ Preserve existing cleanup markers, cleanup verification, error aggregation, and subject ownership behavior.
    ☐ Keep ordinary class mounts free of TestBed allocation, adapter calls, and lifecycle changes.

11.4 Focused mount verification:
    ☐ Update command, mount-context, component classification, resource-ownership, and cleanup-verification specs.
    ☐ Test class success and failure paths with a spy proving zero TestBeds are allocated.
    ☐ Test module and script descriptors with omitted and explicit configuration.
    ☐ Test a fresh bed per descriptor mount and no state sharing across consecutive mounts.
    ☐ Test invalid instances, invalid descriptors, invalid TestBed configurations, third class arguments, instantiated TestBeds, missing exports, invalid exports, loader failures, constructor failures, mount failures, assertion failures, and cleanup failures.
    ☐ Inject TestBed-construction and live-adapter setup failures; prove partially constructed core resources are discarded and no unowned cleanup registration remains.
    ☐ Assert exact close counts and component-before-bed teardown ordering on every applicable path.

Phase 12: Complete unit, declaration, and framework integration coverage
    ☐ Exit with deterministic coverage for the entire contract under Lua 5.4 and no dependence on live DFHack for core behavior.

12.1 Test fixture matrix:
    ☐ Add conventional modules for state isolation, nested dependencies, false returns, nil returns, explicit package.loaded writes, published cycles, unpublished cycles, failures, and retries.
    ☐ Add annotated scripts for state isolation, guards, nested reqscript, circular imports, aliases, failures, and retries.
    ☐ Add module and script provider fixtures for every strategy and same-name cross-namespace cases.
    ☐ Add dynamic loader fixtures for load, loadfile, dofile, explicit environments, and multiple returns.
    ☐ Keep fixtures consumer-shaped and separate from implementation internals.

12.2 Focused suites:
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/config_spec.lua for configuration normalization and validation.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/paths_spec.lua for module and script path behavior.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/providers_spec.lua for provider strategy and alias behavior.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/base_environment_spec.lua for globals, facades, reserved fields, and dynamic-loader bindings.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/package_state_spec.lua for private package, require, loader data, cache, and cycle behavior.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/script_loader_spec.lua for reqscript behavior.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/testbed/testbed_spec.lua for public construction, isolation, lifecycle, and entry-point dependency boundaries.
    ☐ Add tests/unit/driver/mount/testbed_adapter_spec.lua and update the existing mount command/context/component/ownership/cleanup specs.
    ☐ Add a host-adapter double that verifies live behavior without importing real DFHack modules into core unit tests.
    ☐ Add regression tests for every proposal discrepancy previously resolved: package mutability, dfhack reservation, BASE_G mutability, native-plugin providers, native Lua cache semantics, and bounded cycles.
    ☐ Run each new focused suite directly before relying on recursive full-suite discovery.

12.3 Declaration checks:
    ☐ Add valid declaration fixtures for zero-argument construction, partial configuration, all provider variants, optional loader data, and both mount overloads.
    ☐ Add invalid declaration fixtures for malformed tokens, nil values, cross-namespace aliases, unknown strategies, descriptor/class overload confusion, component instances, and TestBed instances passed to mount.
    ☐ Run declaration checks against the source-tree Lua language server configuration in this phase.
    ☐ Verify the runtime validator and declared schema agree on every accepted and rejected shape.
    ☐ Defer installed-rock declaration verification until Phase 13 has created and installed the exact artifact.

12.4 Full offline gates:
    ☐ Run tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1 under the repository's Lua 5.4 toolchain.
    ☐ Run tools/Check-Lua.ps1.
    ☐ Record Lua 5.3 as supported without claiming a dedicated conformance result.
    ☐ Do not infer live behavior from these offline results.

Phase 13: Prove the installed-rock downstream boundary
    ☐ Exit with a generated rock that contains the complete public/runtime contract and drives an empty-tree consumer without checkout path leakage.

13.1 Archive construction and audit:
    ☐ Ensure the rockspec packages src/dwarfspec/testbed.lua and every internal module beneath src/dwarfspec/testbed/.
    ☐ Ensure the shipped declaration surface includes canonical TestBed types and tagged mount overloads.
    ☐ Ensure no DwarfSpec tests, fixtures, checkout-only helpers, or source-path assumptions enter the rock.
    ☐ Extend or add package verification that lists the archive and fails when any required TestBed runtime or declaration file is absent.
    ☐ Run tools/Publish.ps1 and retain the exact generated rock as the artifact for all downstream proofs.
    ☐ Keep publication blocked until the archive, Lua 5.4, installed declarations, empty-tree offline, and same-rock live gates all pass.

13.2 Empty-tree offline consumer:
    ☐ Create a disposable LuaRocks tree containing only the generated DwarfSpec rock and its declared dependencies.
    ☐ Keep the DwarfSpec checkout, sibling repositories, and ad hoc package.path entries off the consumer Lua path.
    ☐ Add consumer-owned conventional modules, a require/mkmodule graph, and annotated scripts under the supported layout.
    ☐ Run Busted from the consumer project root and require dwarfspec.testbed from the installed rock.
    ☐ Exercise zero-config loading, a stateful require/mkmodule graph, module and script replacements, aliases, globals.dfhack, dynamic loaders, and close behavior.
    ☐ Run installed declaration checks from the same empty-tree consumer boundary.
    ☐ Record paths and loaded-module evidence proving the checkout was not used.

13.3 Artifact identity:
    ☐ Hash or otherwise uniquely identify the rock used by the offline consumer.
    ☐ Carry that exact artifact forward to live validation without rebuilding it.

Phase 14: Prove live descriptor mounting with the same installed rock
    ☐ Exit with bounded DFHack automation demonstrating real component construction, interaction, cleanup, and fresh graph state from the packaged artifact.

14.1 Representative downstream component:
    ☐ Use at least one production-style consumer widget or component, not only synthetic TestBed fixtures.
    ☐ Give the component a real module dependency and an annotated script dependency.
    ☐ Provide at least one dependency replacement through TestBed configuration before the defining component source loads.
    ☐ Exercise the synthesized foundational host providers and exact DFHack class identity.
    ☐ Add a second case with component_imports=false or an explicit provider override.

14.2 Live automation cases:
    ☐ Install the exact rock identified in Phase 13 into the live test environment.
    ☐ Run one focused forward-slash automation spec/glob per invocation with a bounded timeout.
    ☐ Prove a default-config module descriptor mount constructs, mounts, interacts, and unmounts.
    ☐ Prove an explicit-config script or module descriptor observes its replacements.
    ☐ Prove two consecutive descriptor mounts observe fresh consumer module and script state.
    ☐ Prove an ordinary class mount still works and allocates no TestBed.
    ☐ Prove an already-created instance and a third argument on a class mount are rejected.
    ☐ Prove an excluded host module fails until explicitly supplied with use_host.
    ☐ Prove a genuine native plugins.* provider can be borrowed, faked, or shimmed as appropriate to the fixture.

14.3 Live failure and cleanup cases:
    ☐ Exercise descriptor resolution failure, constructor failure, mount failure, assertion failure, and cleanup failure.
    ☐ Verify each mount-owned bed closes exactly once.
    ☐ Verify component teardown precedes bed teardown whenever a component was mounted.
    ☐ Verify the host remains usable and no active component, screen, or owned run resource remains.
    ☐ Inspect the terminal run result and structured archive/log evidence; do not treat READY or process exit alone as success.
    ☐ Record cleanup_confirmed or the repository's equivalent explicit cleanup signal for each run.

Phase 15: Document, audit, and hand off
    ☐ Exit only when the shipped behavior, documentation, artifacts, evidence, and repository state satisfy the full implementation contract.

15.1 User documentation:
    ☐ Document zero-config standalone use and configured module/script replacements.
    ☐ Document tagged module and script descriptor mounts with the separate constructor-options and TestBed-config arguments.
    ☐ Document all provider strategies, token namespaces, defaults, override precedence, and borrowed host effects.
    ☐ Document the DFHack-version mapping for the synthesized component-import set and the versioned reserved-loader policy.
    ☐ Document private package mutability, native Lua compatibility, the bounded-cycle exception, and Lua 5.3 versus 5.4 return differences.
    ☐ Document dfhack and BASE_G facade behavior, globals.dfhack completeness, protected loader operations, and explicit host escape hatches.
    ☐ Document lifecycle ownership and the fact that values returned before close remain ordinary references.
    ☐ State clearly that TestBed is not a security sandbox and does not undo native or borrowed-state effects.
    ☐ Publish the proposal's isolation-guarantee table or an equivalent precise boundary matrix in user-facing documentation.
    ☐ Warn against loading the same consumer source both globally and through a TestBed in one example because class and singleton identities will differ.
    ☐ Warn against loading foundational DFHack framework modules from source roots; use synthesized or explicit host providers when real class identity is required.
    ☐ Explain that borrowed host modules retain host-resolved nested dependencies and cannot be retroactively changed by TestBed-local replacements.
    ☐ Document debug, borrowed native functions, external preload functions, and replacement searchers as explicit escapes from environment rewriting.
    ☐ State clearly that diagnostic graph inspection and reload APIs are deferred.

15.2 Contract audit:
    ☐ Re-read later sections of docs/testbed-proposal.md before reporting any discrepancy or omission.
    ☐ Trace every proposal acceptance criterion to implementation, a focused test, and the appropriate evidence gate.
    ☐ Search for obsolete TestBedInput, sources, modules, reusable TestBed mount instances, component instance mounts, path-containment claims, legacy moduleMode, native-plugin rejection, immutable BASE_G, and no-active-bed requirements.
    ☐ Search for accidental DFHack/live imports reachable from require('dwarfspec.testbed').
    ☐ Search for undocumented public TestBed functions or graph-inspection surfaces.
    ☐ Audit every new or changed callable API for language-standard documentation comments.

15.3 Final verification:
    ☐ Re-run all focused TestBed and mount specs.
    ☐ Re-run tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1.
    ☐ Re-run tools/Check-Lua.ps1.
    ☐ Re-run source and installed declaration checks.
    ☐ Re-run package creation and required-file archive inspection.
    ☐ Re-run the empty-tree downstream Busted proof using the final rock.
    ☐ Re-run the focused live downstream component proof using that same rock.
    ☐ Record exact commands, exit statuses, artifact identity, live terminal results, and cleanup confirmation.

15.4 Repository cleanup and handoff:
    ☐ Remove temporary package trees, generated fixtures, logs, and installed live-test artifacts that are not intentional deliverables.
    ☐ Preserve required evidence artifacts in the documented repository location.
    ☐ Review git status and diff; separate intended TestBed changes from pre-existing unrelated work.
    ☐ Confirm no generated rock, runtime cache, or temporary consumer path is accidentally tracked unless explicitly required.
    ☐ Update this checklist with final evidence and leave no checked item whose gate was not actually observed.

Definition of done:
    ☐ A standalone consumer can require dwarfspec.testbed from the installed rock and obtain private bed-owned caches and fresh source-loaded module and script state under Lua 5.4.
    ☐ The canonical TestBed types are usable and correctly checked from the installed artifact.
    ☐ Provider, package, environment, dfhack facade, cycle, dynamic-loader, reqscript, and close semantics match the approved proposal.
    ☐ Ordinary class mounts remain two-argument and TestBed-free; tagged descriptors create, use, and close one fresh mount-owned TestBed.
    ☐ Unit, syntax, declaration, package, downstream offline, and downstream live gates all pass independently.
    ☐ Live evidence confirms interaction and cleanup, not merely module loading or process readiness.
    ☐ No deferred feature or obsolete requirement has been reintroduced.

